# Mutation cases for §scxml-3.13 / Appendix D — an event handed to a machine
# that has already stopped is never looked at, and the host can find out.
#
# Run with `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/unseen_event_is_reported.cases`.
#
# Appendix D's main event loop exits when the machine reaches a top-level final
# state. Refusing what arrives afterwards is the clause; saying nothing about it
# is not. The silence is expensive because it is shaped exactly like the two
# outcomes a host can already read — all three leave the configuration alone:
#
#   dequeued, no transition matched            `discarded_external_events`
#   dequeued, matched, guard said no           nothing, correctly
#   never dequeued — the machine had stopped   the count these cases pin
#
# The trap they exist for is the third one below. There are TWO refusal sites,
# not one — `process_event` refuses before queueing, and the loop abandons what
# is already queued — so a fix applied to either alone leaves a host that drives
# the engine the other way still unable to tell. A case removes each.
#
# The C++ engines and the C11 template halves live in
# `unseen_event_is_reported_ctest.cases`.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_tests -p sce-rust-tests --test unseen_event_is_reported
mutation_targets backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs

mutation_oracles backends/rust/tests/tests/unseen_event_is_reported.rs

# ── The count itself ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The engine goes back to refusing silently. Every driver's first assertion is
# this one.

mutation_case "the loop abandons the queue without recording it" <<'PY'
# The call is kept behind a false guard rather than deleted: `warnings = deny`
# turns an unused private method into a compile error, and a mutation that will
# not build is INCONCLUSIVE rather than a verdict. Measured — deleting it
# outright produced exactly that.
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """                self.record_unseen_external_events();
                break;""",
     """                if false {
                    self.record_unseen_external_events();
                }
                break;""")
PY

# ── The other refusal site ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `process_event` never queues at all when the machine has stopped, so the
# loop's drain cannot see that delivery. A host driving the engine through this
# door reads zero while its events vanish.

mutation_case "the convenience door refuses without recording it" <<'PY'
# Deletes the RECORD, keeping the early return. Disabling the whole guard
# instead — `if !self.is_running && false` — is an EQUIVALENT MUTANT and was
# measured as one: control then falls through to `raise_external` + `step`, the
# loop breaks on the same `!is_running`, and `record_unseen_external_events`
# counts the very same delivery. 0 of 5 tests red, twice, because the two
# programs are observably identical. The defect this axis is about is the
# refusal that records NOTHING, which is this edit.
#
# `note_unseen_event` stays used — the loop's drain calls it — so the tree
# still compiles under `warnings = deny`.
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """            self.note_unseen_event(event);
            return;
        }
        self.raise_external(event, "", "");""",
     """            return;
        }
        self.raise_external(event, "", "");""")
PY

# ── Counting the wrong fact ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The defect this axis is really about: reporting a refusal as a discard. The
# two are different facts — the first says the machine never looked, the second
# says it looked and nothing matched — and a host acts differently on each.

mutation_case "a refusal is reported as a discard" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """        self.unseen_external_events = self.unseen_external_events.saturating_add(1);
        self.last_unseen_event = Some(event);""",
     """        self.discarded_external_events = self.discarded_external_events.saturating_add(1);
        self.last_unseen_event = Some(event);""")
PY

# ── An always-true count ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A count that also fires while the machine is running is exactly as useless as
# one that never fires, and only the assertions taken BEFORE `finish` catch it.

mutation_case "every delivery is counted as unseen" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """    pub fn raise_external(&mut self, event: P::Event, event_data: &str, origin: &str) {""",
     """    pub fn raise_external(&mut self, event: P::Event, event_data: &str, origin: &str) {
        self.note_unseen_event(event);""")
PY

# ── The queue is left in place ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# Counting without draining re-counts the same queue on every later `step()`,
# so the number grows while nothing arrives. Only the assertion that pins the
# count at exactly 1 after one refused delivery catches this.

mutation_case "the abandoned queue is counted but not drained" <<'PY'
# `len()` is deliberately NOT on `EventQueueLike` — the engine's dependency is
# `raise` / `pop` / `has_events` only — so the re-count defect is expressed with
# a single pop-and-put-back. A `while` around the same body would not terminate.
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """        while let Some(meta) = self.external_queue.pop() {
            self.note_unseen_event(meta.event);
        }""",
     """        if let Some(meta) = self.external_queue.pop() {
            self.note_unseen_event(meta.event);
            self.external_queue.raise(meta);
        }""")
PY

# ── Naming the event ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The count says an event went unlooked-at; a host debugging a supervisor that
# stopped answering needs the name. Leaving it at `None` still counts correctly.

mutation_case "the refused event is counted but never named" <<'PY'
# `event` stays referenced: dropping it makes the parameter unused, which
# `warnings = deny` turns into a compile error, and a mutation that will not
# build produces no verdict at all.
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """        self.last_unseen_event = Some(event);""",
     """        self.last_unseen_event = if true { None } else { Some(event) };""")
PY
